1. Volume (Q2) — summary records, not one per write. vf.measure / stress.start write ONE fsync'd `runtime_attempt` record BEFORE the first cpufreq/EPP write,
   listing every file the run will touch with its original value (the same content the measure/stress journal holds) and the planned caps; and ONE fsync'd
   `result` record at the end (also on cancel/error) with: restored ok / per-file restore failures, duration, points measured. A write or restore that FAILS gets
   its own record (event `error`, file, value, reason). No per-successful-write records. Keep the AUDIT_UNAVAILABLE refusal before the first change.
   Update test_g235.py: audit count no longer equals the write count; assert start record lists every touched file + original, result after, failure records.
2. recover_journal at helper start (core.py hook; ops_measure/ops_stress/ops_runtime journal recovery, Q4): the same — one record before restoring (files +
   values), one result. You may edit those recovery functions for this only.
3. Audit screen (Q1) — you may now edit desktop/app/ui/views/audit.js: `runtime_attempt` and `result` records show under "Writes & refusals"; the detail
   column shows `o.detail` when present (fallback reason/purpose); a measure/stress summary row shows "<op>: N files, restored ok" style text. node tests.
4. Q3 (stress.start without a cap refuses on an unwritable audit log): accepted — it writes a result file; keep it.
